<article class="env-proposition" id="ED450E">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/ED450E">Proposition <span data-tag="ED450E">7.2.2.12</span>.</a> Let $\calX $ be an $\infty $-topos and $n \geq 0$ a nonnegative integer, and let $\pi _ n: \calX _{\ast } \rightarrow \Nerve (\Disc (\calX ))$ denote the associated homotopy group functor. </p>
  <p>Then: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
If $n = 0$, then $\pi _ n$ determines an equivalence from the $\infty $-category $\EM _{0}(\calX )$ to the (nerve of the) category of pointed objects of $\Disc (\calX )$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
If $n = 1$, then $\pi _ n$ determines an equivalence from the $\infty $-category $\EM _{1}(\calX )$ to the (nerve of the) category of group objects of $\Disc (\calX )$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
If $n \geq 2$, then $\pi _ n$ determines an equivalence from the $\infty $-category $\EM _{n}(\calX )$ to the (nerve of the) category of commutative group objects of $\Disc (\calX )$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We use induction on $n$. The case $n=0$ follows immediately from the definitions. The case $n=1$ follow from the case $n=0$, by combining Lemmas <a href="/tag/943995" data-tag="943995">7.2.2.11</a> and <a href="/tag/1B3334" data-tag="1B3334">7.2.2.10</a>. If $n=2$, we apply the inductive hypothesis, together with Lemma <a href="/tag/943995" data-tag="943995">7.2.2.11</a> and the observation that if $\calC $ is an ordinary category which admits finite products, then $\Group ( \Group (\calC ))$ is equivalent to category $\Ab (\calC )$ of <em>commutative</em> group objects of $\calC $. The argument in the case $n > 2$ makes use of the inductive hypothesis, Lemma <a href="/tag/943995" data-tag="943995">7.2.2.11</a>, and the observation that $\Group ( \Ab (\calC ) )$ is equivalent to $\Ab (\calC )$ for any ordinary category $\calC $ which admits finite products. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>